
=== mocking ===
  domain      : neighborhood fundraiser baking
  relationship: longtime friends and volunteer partners
  context     : A and B are responsible for bringing sixty pastries to a neighborhood fundraiser at 8 a.m. tomorrow. It is already 8 p.m., and B's oven heats unevenly unless it is watched closely. A has attempted macarons twice before; one batch spread into a single sheet and the other cracked before it could be packed.
  A proposes  : Let's make sixty macarons from scratch tonight instead of buying cookies.
  why         : The proposal collides with the short deadline, unreliable oven, and A's established record with this exact difficult pastry, so disbelief is more natural than warm shared amusement; there is no fortunate payoff or longstanding plan being fulfilled.
  A should    : insist on seriousness and justify the plan
  gold        : I'm serious—I borrowed the café's convection oven, and I already weighed everything out.

=== genuine ===
  domain      : community garden harvest contest
  relationship: next-door neighbors who share a garden plot
  context     : A and B have entered the neighborhood harvest contest for the past two years with pumpkins small enough to carry in one hand. Each time, they painted the pumpkins gold and put a paper ribbon on them that said “Champion.” This week, their shared plot produced a 38-pound pumpkin, and the contest check-in is Saturday morning.
  A proposes  : Let’s put it in the little red wagon and enter our champion on Saturday.
  why         : The oversized pumpkin makes their old paper-ribbon joke unexpectedly real, so B’s laughter naturally treats the proposal as a shared payoff and agreement. Derision is less natural because the proposal is practical and the contest is imminent.
  A should    : move to the practical next step
  gold        : Great, I’ll bring the wagon around after work.

